# Frontend Standards

The Angular client lives in `ntier-template-web/` at the repo root — kebab-case, visually separate from PascalCase backend projects.

## Stack

- **Angular 21** (stable) — pin CLI with `@angular/cli@21`; do not use `@latest` (resolves to Angular 22)
- **Angular Material** `@21` for UI components and layout
- **NgRx** `21.1.x` (`@ngrx/store`, `@ngrx/effects`, `@ngrx/store-devtools`) — minor version must match installed NgRx line; major must match Angular
- In `package.json`, use **minor-version wildcards** (e.g. `21.2.x`, `7.8.x`) — never `^` or `~`
- **Standalone components** with separate template and stylesheet files; never embed SCSS or HTML in the component TypeScript file

## Component Prefix

- Template source uses selector prefix **`ntier`** (e.g. `ntier-home`)
- On `dotnet new`, prefix is replaced with **lowercase initials** derived from the `-n` name (PascalCase capitals — e.g. `DietzMoss` → `dm`)
- Override with `--webPrefixOverride <prefix>` when initials are not suitable

## SCSS / CSS

- Never use `rgba()` for colors; always use 8-digit hex `#rrggbbaa` (e.g. `#00000040` for black at 25% opacity)
- Sort **declarations** lexically (A–Z by property name) within each rule block — applies to `.scss`, `.css`, and `<style>` blocks
- Sort map keys lexically when authoring SCSS maps (e.g. theme config)
- Nested rule blocks each get their own sort; `@use`, `@include`, and nested selectors are not reorderable declarations

```scss
// ✅ GOOD
.card
{
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: #000000;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem;
}

// ❌ BAD — properties out of order
.card
{
    padding: 1rem;
    color: #000000;
    background-color: #ffffff;
}
```

## TypeScript / General

- Always use braces `{ }` for all control structures, even single-line blocks
- Always use TypeScript over JavaScript and SCSS over CSS whenever possible
- Avoid dynamic `import()` wherever possible; prefer static imports. When a circular dependency appears, break the cycle (e.g. split UI orchestration from domain/session logic into another injectable) rather than lazy-loading modules to hide the cycle. Intentional route-level code splitting (`loadComponent`, `loadChildren`) is fine
- For documentation comments, see `documentation-standards.mdc`
- For template binding rules, see `angular-template-simplicity.mdc`
